# sudoers rules for the Xymon "smart" extension.
#
# smartctl needs raw device access; the Xymon client usually runs as
# user "xymon". Install this file with visudo -f:
#
#   Linux:    visudo -f /etc/sudoers.d/xymon-smart
#   FreeBSD:  visudo -f /usr/local/etc/sudoers.d/xymon-smart
#
# Every rule below is commented out: uncomment the lines of ONE
# variant, with your platform's smartctl path (Linux /usr/sbin/smartctl,
# FreeBSD /usr/local/sbin/smartctl).
#
# What smart.sh runs as root, always with the options in this order:
#
#   smartctl --version                         sudo probe
#   smartctl --scan                            device discovery
#   smartctl -i -H -A [-n standby] [OPTS] DEV  one call per device
#   mmc extcsd read DEV                        eMMC (Linux, mmc-utils)
#
# "-n standby" is there with NOSPINUP="yes" (the default). OPTS is what
# a device line in smart.cfg adds, e.g. device /dev/sdb "-d sat".
#
# Pitfalls:
#
# - A command WITHOUT an argument list may be run with ANY arguments
#   (sudoers(5)): "smartctl -s off", "-t long", "-X", "--set=..." -
#   that is variant C, not limited to read-only queries.
# - A "*" in the arguments matches white space as well, i.e. further
#   arguments: "smartctl -i -H -A *" also allows
#   "smartctl -i -H -A -s off /dev/sda". It restricts nothing; neither
#   does "mmc extcsd read /dev/mmcblk*" restrict the device.
# - Regular expressions (variant A) need sudo 1.9.10 or later. Older
#   sudo reads "^...$" as a plain pattern that matches nothing, so the
#   column stays clear ("passwordless sudo ... is not configured").
#   sudo-rs supports neither regular expressions nor wildcards inside
#   the arguments (sudoers-rs(5)): use variant B there.
# - Options that variant A does not know (anything in OPTS other than
#   "-d TYPE") need a line of their own, e.g. from variant B.
#
# sudo by platform (packages as of 2026-11; check with "sudo -V"):
#
#   Ubuntu 22.04          sudo 1.9.9                      -> B
#   Ubuntu 24.04          sudo 1.9.15p5                   -> A
#   Ubuntu 26.04          sudo-rs 0.2.13 is /usr/bin/sudo -> B
#                         (sudo 1.9.17p2 is installed too, as sudo.ws;
#                         A only after switching the alternative)
#   EL 8                  sudo 1.9.5p2                    -> B
#   EL 9                  sudo 1.9.5p2; CentOS Stream 9 moved to
#                         1.9.17p2 in 2025-11             -> B, or A
#                         once "sudo -V" says 1.9.10+
#   EL 10                 sudo 1.9.17p2                   -> A
#   FreeBSD (ports/pkg)   sudo 1.9.17p2                   -> A

# --- Variant A: regular expressions (sudo 1.9.10 or later) ----------
# Only the calls smart.sh makes, for any disk the scan finds and any
# smartctl device type ("-d sat", "-d megaraid,0", ...).

# Linux:
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl ^--(version|scan)$
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl ^-i -H -A( -n standby)?( -d [a-z0-9,+/]+)? /dev/(sd[a-z]+|nvme[0-9]+(n[0-9]+)?)$
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mmc ^extcsd read /dev/mmcblk[0-9]+$

# FreeBSD:
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/smartctl ^--(version|scan)$
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/smartctl ^-i -H -A( -n standby)?( -d [a-z0-9,+/]+)? /dev/(ada[0-9]+|da[0-9]+|nda[0-9]+|nvme[0-9]+)$

# --- Variant B: exact arguments (every sudo, sudo-rs included) -------
# One line per disk, written exactly as smart.sh calls smartctl for it:
# list the disks with "smartctl --scan"; without "-n standby" if
# NOSPINUP="no"; with the OPTS of its device line in smart.cfg. Outside
# a regular expression, "," ":" "=" and "\" must be escaped with "\"
# ("-d megaraid\,0"). sdX names can change between boots - adjust the
# lines when they do, or name the disks in smart.cfg.

#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl --version, /usr/sbin/smartctl --scan
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl -i -H -A -n standby /dev/sda
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl -i -H -A -n standby /dev/nvme0
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl -i -H -A -n standby -d sat /dev/sdb
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mmc extcsd read /dev/mmcblk0

# --- Variant C: any arguments (what this file shipped up to 0.35.1) --
# Simple, but grants every smartctl function as root, see above.

# Debian/Ubuntu and Rocky/EL:
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/sbin/smartctl

# FreeBSD (smartmontools from ports/packages):
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/smartctl

# Linux hosts with eMMC devices (the subcommand is fixed, the "*" lets
# the device path run on):
#xymon ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/mmc extcsd read /dev/mmcblk*
